  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The lead they held dissolved in one inning, and they never built another.",
    "what_this_means": "Jack Perkins kept this game close through 5.2 innings on two earned runs. The offense, though, wasted the runway. Six hits and thirteen strikeouts against a Boston team that wasn't overpowering anyone. Colby Thomas was the only hitter who found anything. The real damage arrived in the sixth: Jos\u00e9 Suarez gave up two runs immediately, the lead collapsed, and the game moved out of reach. By the ninth, with two runners in scoring position and nothing to show for it, the window had closed. This is a team that creates chances and forgets to use them.",
    "what_to_watch": "Inconsistency isn't new to this roster, but it's getting louder. They led through five innings because the pitching held and the hitting found enough. Then one inning erased all of that. The pattern is straightforward now: the Athletics build something. A lead, a moment, a small edge. And watch it slip away when they're not executing behind it. They're 44-62 and ten games back. Games like this don't reverse that math. They confirm it.",
